  "answer": "ARP Spoofing works by an adversary intercepting ARP requests on a network. The attacker waits for a device to request the MAC address associated with a specific IP address. When this happens, the attacker responds with their own MAC address instead of the legitimate one, tricking the requesting device into believing it is communicating with the intended device. This process is known as \"poisoning\" the ARP cache. For the attack to be successful, the adversary's reply must arrive faster than the legitimate device's response. Additionally, attackers may send gratuitous ARP replies to falsely claim ownership of an IP address [Source 2].",
